  "ai_show_review": {
    "summary": "Historic debut‑laden show with soaring Eyes of the World",
    "blurb": "The Stanford ’73 concert bursts with first‑time material and a monster 18‑minute Eyes Of The World. Jerry’s guitar is crisp and fat, while Bill’s kick drives a tight rhythm section. Highlights include the Truckin’ → Eyes → China Doll segue and killer renditions of Here Comes Sunshine and Loose Lucy.",
    "review": "On February 9, 1973 at Roscoe Maples Pavilion the Dead unveiled a slew of new songs that would become staples. The first set feels like a warm‑up but already hints at the fireworks to come, with Row Jimmy opening strong and a tight groove on Deal and Sugaree. The second set is where the night truly shines: a blistering China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider transition launches into Jack Straw, then they launch into an unforgettable Truckin’ → Eyes Of The World (a soaring 18‑minute jam) → China Doll sequence that many reviewers call “high‑energy” and “seamless.” Later, the band delivers crisp renditions of Big River, Wave That Flag, Sugar Magnolia and a joyous Uncle John’s Band before closing with a classic Casey Jones encore. Across all recordings Jerry’s tone is described as “pristine,” “fat,” and “blazing,” front‑and‑center in the mix; Phil’s bass shines especially on the funkier numbers, while Bob’s rhythm work is “tight” and “never better.” Bill Kreutzmann’s kick drum provides a clear, punchy pulse that carries the set, and Keith Godchaux adds depth despite occasional mix burying. The audience tape mastered by Ashley is lauded for its crystal‑clear sound, making it the most accessible way to hear this landmark performance.",
